-- PartialOrd / WeakOrd / Ord — the ordering tower (library proposal 004, WI-644;
-- split three ways by WI-1109).
--
-- THREE FLOORS, ONE QUESTION EACH. The tower used to have two, and `Ord` answered two
-- questions at once — "is every pair comparable?" and "is the order's kernel `Eq`?" —
-- which are independent, and a witness that separates them ships in this repo.
--
--   PartialOrd  gt/lt/gte/lte. NO totality: a `NaN` operand answers `false` (IEEE
--               unordered). `Float` provides this and nothing above it.
--   WeakOrd     `compare -> Int64`. TOTAL — every pair is comparable — and CONGRUENT
--               with respect to `Eq`: `eq(a, b)` implies `compare(a, b) = 0`. Its
--               kernel may be strictly COARSER than `Eq`, so it partitions the carrier
--               into equivalence CLASSES. A comparator chosen by a key ("by length",
--               "by first component") lives exactly here.
--   Ord         adds ONLY the converse law — `compare(a, b) = 0` implies `eq(a, b)`,
--               i.e. the kernel IS `Eq`. No new operation, the way `Eq` adds only
--               reflexivity over `PartialEq`. `Int64`, `String`, `BigInt` are here.
--
-- WHY "WEAK" AND NOT "PRE": a preorder need not be total and this one is; "weak order"
-- is the standard name for a TOTAL preorder. The three floors are one-to-one with
-- C++20 `<compare>` — `partial_ordering` (NaN), `weak_ordering` (equivalence WITHOUT
-- substitutability), `strong_ordering` (equivalent implies substitutable) — which is
-- not merely cosmetic: a carrier's floor is what decides the comparison category its
-- generated `operator<=>` returns.
--
-- WHAT SEPARATES THE TOP TWO, concretely. `ByLength` (wi844) compares `String` by
-- length: `compare("zz", "aa") = 0` while `eq("zz", "aa")` is false. It is a lawful
-- `WeakOrd[String]` and NOT an `Ord[String]`. A `SortedSet` under it stores classes —
-- {"zz","aa","b"} has two elements, and `union` keeps the LEFT operand's
-- representative, so it is not commutative. That behaviour is correct for a weak
-- order and is why `SortedSet` requires `WeakOrd`; a consumer wanting no silent
-- collapse requires `Ord` and gets it checked.
--
-- `Float`'s order is partial (NaN unordered), so `Float` provides neither `WeakOrd`
-- nor `Ord`; `TotalFloat` provides `Eq` but still not an order (a functional total
-- float order needs host support — see totalfloat.anthill).
sort anthill.prelude.PartialOrd
  import anthill.prelude.{Int64, WeakOrd}

  sort T = ?
  requires PartialEq[T]

  -- WI-876: these four carry the DERIVATION from `compare` as real default BODIES,
  -- not only as the laws below. Until WI-876 they could not: an eval builtin
  -- registered on these very spec ops shadowed every carrier, so the derivation was
  -- unreachable and each ordered carrier had to restate four one-liners. With the
  -- host implementations keyed per carrier (`operation_map`, proposal 038 binding
  -- blocks), a carrier that supplies `compare` alone inherits the whole surface.
  --
  -- WHY THEY LIVE HERE AND NOT ON `WeakOrd`, where the laws are: `gt`/`gte`/`lt`/`lte`
  -- are DECLARED here, and a spec op's default body must sit with its declaration.
  -- Declaring them a second time above would give a carrier that provides BOTH specs
  -- two `sort_ops` entries for one short name, and which one wins is HashMap-iteration
  -- order (`build_sort_ops_table` pass 2) — a coin flip, not a rule.
  --
  -- ONE COST, recorded rather than hidden. The op-scoped `requires WeakOrd[T]` is
  -- load-bearing for the BODY, and its CALL-SITE half is currently MASKED:
  -- `check_one_spec_op_requirement` returns early for a builtin functor, and
  -- `PartialOrd.gt` is still a resolver builtin. MEASURED (WI-876) — a generic
  -- operation with `requires PartialOrd[T]`, and even one with no `requires` at all,
  -- calls `PartialOrd.gt(a, b)` and loads clean. WHEN WI-879 DELETES THE SPEC-OP
  -- RESOLVER REGISTRATION THAT MASK GOES, and every `PartialOrd`-only generic caller
  -- starts being asked for a `WeakOrd[T]` it has no reason to have. That comes due
  -- there, not here.
  --
  -- (The SECOND cost this note used to carry is GONE with WI-1109. It read: the
  -- derivation reads a `compare` that `PartialOrd` does not require, so a carrier
  -- providing `PartialOrd` ALONE inherits a body that cannot run for it, and the load
  -- check calls it "backed". The op-scoped `requires WeakOrd[T]` above is now exactly
  -- the per-provision condition 058 §3.8 named as the proper statement of it.)
  --
  -- The inner comparison is `Int64.gt` EXPLICITLY, not the unqualified `gt` the laws
  -- write: unqualified inside this very operation is `PartialOrd.gt` itself, i.e. this
  -- body calling itself on `compare`'s `Int64` result. Naming `Int64` bottoms the
  -- recursion out at that carrier's own host implementation in one hop, and hard-wires
  -- nothing that `compare`'s declared `-> Int64` return does not already fix.
  operation {
    gt(a: T, b: T) -> Bool requires WeakOrd[T]          -- >
      = Int64.gt(WeakOrd.compare(a, b), 0)
    gte(a: T, b: T) -> Bool requires WeakOrd[T]         -- >=
      = Int64.gte(WeakOrd.compare(a, b), 0)
    lt(a: T, b: T) -> Bool requires WeakOrd[T]          -- <
      = Int64.lt(WeakOrd.compare(a, b), 0)
    lte(a: T, b: T) -> Bool requires WeakOrd[T]         -- <=
      = Int64.lte(WeakOrd.compare(a, b), 0)
  }
end

-- WeakOrd: the TOTAL order whose kernel may be coarser than `Eq`. This is the floor
-- that sorting actually needs, and the one a chosen comparator provides.
sort anthill.prelude.WeakOrd
  import anthill.prelude.Int64.{neg}
  -- WI-894: `ite` is a RULE-INTRODUCED functor on `Bool`, and a rule does not
  -- travel to the global namespace from its place — so the `max`/`min` laws
  -- below reach `bool.anthill`'s `[simp]` rules by THIS import, not by the name
  -- being global. Resolves through the deferred-import retry (WI-295): a rule
  -- head's symbol is registered in scan pass 3, after imports are processed.
  import anthill.prelude.Bool.{ite}

  sort T = ?

  -- `Eq` is the RELATUM, not something this spec supplies. The congruence law below
  -- is stated against it, so it has to exist — but a derived `Eq` would be a SECOND
  -- supplier for the carrier, and equality dispatches from UNIFICATION (`sem_eq_core`,
  -- kb/resolve.rs) where no call site exists to select at, so `AmbiguousEqDispatch`
  -- (WI-837) refuses one. Hence: required, never provided. (WI-456 asked whether a
  -- local `Eq` should be derived from the order; this is the answer, and the reason
  -- it does not contradict the `provides` links elsewhere in this tower is that a
  -- PROOF IS NOT A SUPPLIER — a law adds no `sort_ops` entry and no dispatch
  -- candidate, so relating an order to `Eq` never mints a rival.)
  requires Eq[T]
  requires PartialOrd[T]

  -- Primary operation: compare returns -1, 0, or 1
  operation compare(a: T, b: T) -> Int64

  -- Derived operations. WI-876: real default BODIES, so a carrier supplying
  -- `compare` inherits them. They were the load check's sharpest inconsistency —
  -- `max`/`min` are not resolver builtins, so the check DEMANDED a member for them
  -- from every concrete ordered carrier, while `gt`/`lt`/`gte`/`lte` ARE, so it
  -- demanded nothing and they broke at eval instead. Two halves of one surface
  -- treated oppositely; both are now backed by the same derivation.
  --
  -- Written as `if`/`then`/`else` rather than the `ite` of the law below: `ite` is not
  -- an operation (WI-887), and `if` is the operation-body conditional.
  --
  -- NOTE for a coarse kernel: `max`/`min` return ONE of two operands that may compare
  -- equal without being equal, and THE FIRST OPERAND WINS BOTH — `gte`/`lte` each hold
  -- at a tie, so the `then` branch is taken and that branch is `a`. Which representative
  -- comes back is therefore observable under `WeakOrd` and not under `Ord`, the same way
  -- `SortedSet.union` is left-biased. Stated so it is contract rather than discovery.
  operation {
    max(a: T, b: T) -> T = if gte(a, b) then a else b
    min(a: T, b: T) -> T = if lte(a, b) then a else b
  }

  -- gt/lt/gte/lte are inherited from PartialOrd; for a total order they relate to
  -- `compare` by these laws.
  --
  -- WI-876: since those six gained real default BODIES, each law now has an
  -- executable twin. The BODY is what runs — a rule is not backing (WI-818) — and
  -- these stay as the SPECIFICATION the bodies are proved against, which is also
  -- what the equational/`[simp]`/proof layers rewrite with. Two spellings of one
  -- definition, deliberately, and they are allowed to differ where the surfaces do:
  -- `max` is stated here with `ite` and written as `if`/`then`/`else` there, because
  -- `ite` is a rule-level functor and `if` is the operation-body form (WI-887).
  rule {
    gt(?a, ?b)  <=> gt(compare(?a, ?b), 0)
    lt(?a, ?b)  <=> lt(compare(?a, ?b), 0)
    gte(?a, ?b) <=> gte(compare(?a, ?b), 0)
    lte(?a, ?b) <=> lte(compare(?a, ?b), 0)
    max(?a, ?b) <=> ite(gte(?a, ?b), ?a, ?b)
    min(?a, ?b) <=> ite(lte(?a, ?b), ?a, ?b)
  }

  -- Laws that hold for ANY total order, coarse kernel or not.
  rule {
    -- antisymmetry
    compare_antisym: compare(?a, ?b) <=> neg(compare(?b, ?a))
    -- reflexivity
    compare_refl: compare(?a, ?a) <=> 0
  }

  -- CONGRUENCE — the law that makes this floor usable, and the only half of the old
  -- `compare_eq` biconditional that is true here. It says `compare` is WELL-DEFINED ON
  -- `Eq`-CLASSES: the kernel of `compare` is a union of `Eq`-classes, never a splitting
  -- of one. That is what keeps a sorted structure well formed — no `Eq`-class can land
  -- in two positions — and it is the weakest law that does.
  --
  -- Written as a `constraint` (the `Int64.div_nonzero` idiom: head must hold wherever
  -- the body does) and NOT as an equation, deliberately. An `<=>` is available to the
  -- equational / `[simp]` / proof layers as a REWRITE, and rewriting `eq(a, b)` to
  -- `eq(compare(a, b), 0)` is exactly the unsound step at a coarse kernel. An
  -- implication states the obligation without minting the rewrite.
  constraint compare_congruent: eq(compare(?a, ?b), 0) :- eq(?a, ?b)
end

-- Ord: the TOTAL order whose kernel IS `Eq` — C++'s `strong_ordering`, Haskell's and
-- Rust's `Ord`. It adds no operation, only the converse of `WeakOrd`'s congruence.
sort anthill.prelude.Ord
  sort T = ?

  -- THE WHOLE CONTENT OF THIS FLOOR, ONE LINE, AND ONE LINE IS ENOUGH (WI-1110).
  -- A carrier providing `Ord` thereby provides `WeakOrd` — ONE `compare`, one order,
  -- with this floor asserting the extra law about it. Deriving beats writing both
  -- floors (058 §3.8): two hand-written provisions permit a bundle whose halves
  -- disagree, whereas one `compare` cannot.
  --
  -- `provides` ON A SPEC IS A CONVERSION, and a conversion is a chain entry: "hold an
  -- `Ord[T]` and you can obtain a `WeakOrd[T]`", which is what `Ord <: WeakOrd` means
  -- operationally. So this one clause does everything the four it replaces did —
  -- a body written against `requires Ord[T]` reaches `WeakOrd.compare` through the
  -- slot this puts in the chain, and `WeakOrd`'s own `requires Eq[T]` and
  -- `requires PartialOrd[T]` travel to the carrier through it, which is why they are no
  -- longer restated here.
  --
  -- WI-1109 SHIPPED THIS AS `requires WeakOrd[T]` + `provides WeakOrd[T = T]` with a
  -- note insisting the two were "not redundant and not circular". They were one edge
  -- written twice: the `provides` half was filed in the PROVIDER table, where it made
  -- `Ord` an answer to every `WeakOrd` goal (measured `WeakOrd[T = Int64]` ->
  -- `[Ord, Int64]`), and the `requires` half then closed the loop over it — a cycle
  -- caught only because the cycle detector ran. A conversion belongs in the chain, not
  -- in a table of what has which type; nothing has type `Ord`.
  provides WeakOrd[T = T]

  -- THE LAW, and with `WeakOrd`'s congruence it says the kernel of `compare` is exactly
  -- `Eq` — which is what makes `compare(a, b) = 0` substitutable and a `SortedSet` under
  -- this floor a set of ELEMENTS rather than of classes.
  --
  -- AN EQUATION HERE, A `constraint` ON `WeakOrd`, and the difference is soundness rather
  -- than style. An `<=>` is what the equational / `[simp]` / proof layers REWRITE with,
  -- and rewriting `eq(a, b)` to `eq(compare(a, b), 0)` is valid exactly where the kernel
  -- IS `Eq` — here, and not one floor down, where a coarse comparator makes it false.
  -- So `WeakOrd` states only the implication it can defend and mints no rewrite, while
  -- `Ord` — whose entire content is that the two coincide — carries the biconditional.
  --
  -- WI-1109 first wrote this side as a `constraint` too, which was over-correction: it
  -- moved the unsound rewrite off `WeakOrd` and then DELETED the sound one, so
  -- `Int64`/`String`/`BigInt` silently lost a law they had had all along and nothing
  -- recorded the loss. Restored, in the spelling the file's other laws use.
  --
  -- Untagged, so it is INERT as a rewrite until a `[simp]` tag enables it (WI-881/884) —
  -- the status `compare_antisym`, `compare_refl` and `eq_refl` all have. What it is FOR
  -- is being the specification those layers prove against.
  rule compare_eq: eq(?a, ?b) <=> eq(WeakOrd.compare(?a, ?b), 0)
end
